WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:08.910
This beautiful scene is from our paper by
the name Gaussian Material Synthesis, and

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:14.480
it contains more than a 100 different materials,
each of which has been learned and synthesized

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.850
by an AI.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:21.300
None of these daisies and dandelions are alike,
each of them have a different material model.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:26.550
Normally, to obtain results like this, an
artist has to engage in a direct interaction

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.980
with an interface that you see here.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.079
This contains a ton of parameters and to be
able to use it properly the artist needs to

00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:38.680
have years of experience in photorealistic
rendering and material modeling.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.559
But, unfortunately, the problem gets even
worse.

00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:47.770
Since a proper light simulation program needs
to create an image with the new material parameters,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:53.500
this initially results in a noisy image that
typically takes 40 to 60 seconds to clear

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:54.500
up.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.730
We have to wait out this 40 to 60 seconds
for every single parameter change that we

00:00:58.730 --> 00:00:59.730
make.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.650
This would take several hours in practical
use cases.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:07.960
The goal of this project was to speed up workflows
like this by teaching an AI the concept of

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:13.120
material models such as metals, minerals and
translucent materials.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.020
With our technique, first, we show the user
a gallery of random materials, who assigns

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:23.770
a score to each of them saying that I liked
this one, I didn't like that one, and get

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:28.740
an AI to learn our preferences and recommend
new materials for us.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:33.710
We also created a neural renderer that replaces
the light simulation program and creates a

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:37.659
near-perfect image of the output in about
4 milliseconds.

00:01:37.659 --> 00:01:42.479
That’s not real time, that’s ten times
faster than real time!

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:44.950
That is very fast and accurate.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:49.510
However, our neural renderer is limited to
this scene that you see here.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:54.289
So, the question is, is it possible to create
something that is a little more general?

00:01:54.289 --> 00:01:58.830
Well, let’s have a look at this new work
that performs something similar that they

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.630
call differentiable rendering.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:07.189
The problem formulation is the following:
we specify a target image that is either rendered

00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:11.450
by a computer program, or even better, a photo.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:16.819
The input is a pitiful approximation of it,
and now, hold on to your papers, because it

00:02:16.819 --> 00:02:23.620
progressively changes the input materials,
textures, and even the geometry to match this

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:24.620
photo.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:26.930
My goodness, even the geometry!

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:30.890
This thing is doing three people’s jobs
when given a target photo.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:35.260
And you haven’t seen the best part here,
because there is an animation that shows how

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.170
the input evolves over time as we run this
technique.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:44.350
As we start out, it almost immediately matches
the material properties and the base shape,

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:49.129
and after that, it refines the geometry to
make sure that the more intricate details

00:02:49.129 --> 00:02:51.400
are also matched properly.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:57.180
As always, some limitations apply, for instance,
area light sources are fine, but it doesn’t

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:02.860
support point light sources, may show problems
in the presence of discontinuities and mirror-like

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:03.860
materials.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:08.450
I cannot wait to see where this ends up a
couple papers down the line and I really hope

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:13.640
this thing takes off - in my opinion, this
is one of the most refreshing and exciting

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.430
ideas in photorealistic rendering research
as of late.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.550
More differentiable rendering papers please!

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:24.540
I would like to stress that there are also
other works on differentiable rendering.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.360
This is not the first one.

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:30.390
However, if you have a closer look at the
paper in the description, you will see that

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.939
it does it better than previous techniques.

00:03:32.939 --> 00:03:38.330
In this series, I try to make you feel how
I feel when I read these papers, and I hope

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:43.080
I have managed this time, but you be the judge,
please let me know in the comments!

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.580
And if this got you excited to learn more
about light transport, I am holding a Master-level

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.230
course on it at the Technical University of
Vienna.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:55.599
This course used to take place behind closed
doors, but I feel that the teachings shouldn't

00:03:55.599 --> 00:04:01.129
only be available for the 20-30 people who
can afford a University education, but they

00:04:01.129 --> 00:04:02.630
should be available for everyone.

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:08.110
So, we recorded the entirety of the course
and it is now available for everyone, free

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:09.110
of charge.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:12.819
If you are interested, have a look at the
video description to watch them.

